description:
Implements the bounded composite portion of ADR-0032 Spec 3. Depends on bd-o1hl for common control-flow/guard semantics and bd-htoo for the closed action-contract extension. This is a pure in-engine slice: it has no effect key, mailbox receipt, host sink, or provider call.

Scope:
- add Action::Reduce with the fixed operations all, any, count, sum, and join;
- resolve a proper list through the canonical ref/template path, optionally select a dot-path key, support a bounded initial accumulator, and render join output through the existing template contract;
- reject non-lists, improper collections, unknown operations, missing key paths, join without a template, and over-limit input loudly with structured invalid-input evidence;
- preserve authored/item order and test empty-list, type, key, accumulator, and output-shape behavior.

Non-goals: a general expression/fold language, open action registration, hidden repair, retry policy, effect journaling, or new kinds/tools. The six-site checklist remains mandatory: enum/type name, contract, execution, graph classification, lint, and tests.

Synchronize toron-facing documentation with ADR-0032 without claiming proposed behavior is shipped. Own toron/AGENTS.md, docs/guides/workflow-engine.mdx, the toron changelog entry if needed, and the shared ~/.agents/skills/toron/SKILL.md workflow section plus its workflow-engine reference when present.

Required behavior:
- link the proposed ADR and state the fixed point as truthful at-least-once orchestration with explicit unknown/reconciliation, not exactly-once;
- distinguish mailbox corr/ReceiptStore lifecycle from workflow effect_key/effect journal and distinguish suspended, terminal, settled, and business completion;
- mark react/call_webhook behavior and pending/terminal lifecycle according to the currently shipped implementation, with proposed gates clearly labeled until their beads land;
- document the closed action vocabulary, staged validation, guard/template loud failures, bounded composites, no_claim_timeout semantics, and host-mode preflight without duplicating the complete ADR;
- verify links and grep-based status checks; do not add MCP tools, Nostr kinds, or stale command claims.

This bead is documentation only. It must not update code to make the documentation pass.

Implements ADR-0032 Spec 6 and the host portion of Spec 1 after bd-i6kc and bd-v139.1. The normalized invocation boundary comes from bd-i6kc; durable pending/effect storage comes from bd-v139.1. Own the sink request/result types, structured approval/spawn pending entries, WorkflowHost drain/resolution loop, existing kind-46011/43001 pending publication, kind-46012 terminal publication, and lifecycle tests; do not redesign SQLite effect storage, provider reconciliation, or flywheel run-ledger code.

Required behavior:
- ApprovalSink, SpawnSink, and VerifySink requests carry run_id, project, workflow/node identity, effect context where applicable, promise/generation, bounded payload, deadline, and evidence references; duplicate observations are idempotent;
- parked entries are durably persisted or deterministically replayable before a run is treated as suspended, and restart recovery does not depend on an in-memory sink;
- the host drains pending work independently of one engine.run await, validates run/project/promise/generation/action identity on resolution, ignores or records stale/wrong resolutions, and never resumes another run;
- suspended is distinct from failed/done; terminal state publication uses one semantic terminal digest and is retryable without duplicate semantic terminals;
- CLI execution rejects approval/spawn-await workflows when no compatible host/resolver exists before parking;
- tests cover duplicate resolution, stale generation, restart recovery, interrupted publication, and no-op-host refusal.

This bead does not provide universal exactly-once effects. The workflow-effect journal, provider idempotency/reconciliation, and compensation evidence are separate work in bd-v139.1/bd-v139.2/bd-v139.3.

Implements ADR-0032 Spec 2 and the contract/identity portions of Spec 3 in toron-workflow. Depends on bd-i6kc for the normalized execution boundary. This bead replaces the former “per-step Turn receipt” scope: it does not own mailbox receipts, workflow-effect storage, host draining, or compensation.

Scope:
- define one exhaustive, co-located ActionContract for every closed Action variant: YAML name, fields, template shapes, normalized form, input/output contract, action class, sink requirement, and effect policy;
- consume the same contract at parse/normalize, lint, and invoke/output-validation gates; unknown fields, unresolved refs, wrong rendered shapes, invalid output, and missing sinks fail with stage/code evidence;
- define ResolvedAction/ActionInvocation normal form and deterministic workflow_digest, node_path, iteration_key, action_digest, and stable effect-key inputs inspired by Jido v3 Executable/Instruction/Flow Identity;
- preserve immutable source input and the first prepared normalized identity so replay cannot silently re-render a different logical effect;
- keep execution policy outside Action callbacks, with no open runtime registry and no Jido/Zoi/Splode dependency;
- exhaustively test action coverage, normalization, input/output validation, canonical serialization, redaction-safe identity, and changed-replay rejection.

Own schema/contract/normalization/identity modules and their tests. Coordinate through the ADR contract rather than editing the control-flow parity, host lifecycle, flywheel, or SQLite journal slices. Do not claim that a stable identity alone provides provider idempotency or exactly-once execution.

Implements ADR-0032 Spec 1 and the action-boundary portion of Spec 2 in toron-workflow/toron-mcp. This bead owns the normalized production invocation seam and exhaustive action-runner honesty; it is intentionally separate from control-flow parity, host lifecycle, and effect-journal storage.

Scope:
- replace production use of the context-free effectful runner with a context-bearing ActionInvocation/ActionOutcome/ActionFailure boundary (a temporary pure-action compatibility adapter is allowed);
- make action partitions exhaustive: engine-owned control flow, named host sinks, pure in-engine actions, and explicit unsupported/deprecated actions;
- delete or wire every silent Ok(Null) arm, including an explicit retain/remove decision and test coverage for react and call_webhook; a retained webhook requires the documented SSRF/redirect/DNS/body/deadline/evidence contract;
- normalize failure stage/code/effect state and map only the frozen ADR-0026 FailureKind set; runner callbacks do not retry;
- reject accidental control-action dispatch to the effect runner and prove advertised action coverage is exhaustive against Action::type_name().

Own the runner/boundary modules and their focused unit tests. If extraction is needed, add a dedicated runner module rather than broadening the host lifecycle. Do not implement graph guards/composites/defaults, sink draining or terminal publication, flywheel routing/ledger changes, SQLite workflow-effect tables, provider reconciliation, or compensation.

The bead is complete only when the honesty-gate tests prove no unsupported action can report success and the boundary contract is consumable by later host/effect beads. It does not claim durable effects or exactly-once behavior.

# Task: Define Bus trait + subscription model

## What
```rust
// governed-by: ADR-0003
trait Bus {
  async fn publish(&self, event: Event) -> Result<PublishOk>;
  async fn subscribe(&self, filter: Filter) -> Result<Subscription>;
  // plus health/liveness for health_check tool
}
```
- Filters mirror NIP-01: authors, kinds, since, until, limit, #e, #p, #t, #l
- Subscription yields stream of events + EOSE-equivalent for historical catch-up on InProcessBus

## Why
Mailbox core and projection depend only on trait — swap InProcess vs Relay without tool changes.

## Tests
Mock/in-memory double for unit tests of mailbox later.

# Task: Backend trait definition

## What
```rust
// governed-by: ADR-0003
#[async_trait]
trait Backend {
  async fn spawn(...) -> Result<PaneId>;
  async fn prompt(...) -> Result<()>;
  async fn wait(...) -> Result<AgentStatus>;
  async fn read(...) -> Result<String>;
  async fn send_keys(...) -> Result<()>;
  async fn events(...) -> Result<EventStream>;
}
```
Plus error types with machine codes.

## Tests
- Trait object compilable; mock backend for mailbox tests if needed

Implements ADR-0032 Specs 3, 4, and the control-flow portion of Spec 7 in toron-workflow/toron-cli. This bead is independently executable from the runner-honesty slice: own control_flow.rs, template.rs, lint.rs, the relevant schema defaults, CLI workflow preflight, and focused tests; do not edit the production effect runner or workflow-host lifecycle except where an interface must be consumed.

Required behavior:
- one guard/ref evaluator for graph, linear-desugared, branch, retry-nested, foreach-nested, and call-workflow steps; false is an explicit skip, while syntax/type/unresolved-ref errors fail loudly and never become an else path or placeholder;
- runtime graph validation rejects cycles, unknown dependencies, and empty-ready-set stalls even when lint is bypassed;
- call_workflow and direct invoke share input-default binding, with provided values winning;
- foreach requires a proper list, parallel requires a valid positive bounded count, and invalid values never coerce to [] or 1;
- one retry coordinator owns bounds/backoff/deadline and reads the enclosing policy; a retryable runtime failure retries the same logical node, not a repair graph;
- Spawn absent no_claim_timeout uses 600 seconds and explicit 0 is the only no-timeout spelling; CLI preflight refuses parked workflows when the required host/resolver is unavailable;
- tests prove every listed degradation and preserve the frozen FailureKind vocabulary.

Non-goals: effect journal/storage, provider idempotency, terminal publication, new kinds/tools, a general expression language, or a hidden repair loop. Do not claim exactly-once or shipped host lifecycle in docs from this bead alone.

Implements the persistence portion of ADR-0032 Spec 5.1 as a standalone foundation. Depends on bd-htoo for canonical workflow/action identity and is the prerequisite for host restart recovery and effect dispatch. Own only the existing SQLite migrations and storage primitives for workflow_runs, workflow_effects current-state/attempt evidence, workflow_pending, terminal-publication evidence, bounded redaction/retention metadata, uniqueness constraints, CAS/fencing helpers, and migration/restart/duplicate-write tests. Do not own provider calls, sink draining, CLI lifecycle, flywheel routing, mailbox ReceiptStore semantics, or compensation execution.

Required behavior:
- preserve the distinction between mailbox receipts and workflow effects;
- persist run/project/workflow/node/iteration/action digest identity, stable effect_key, attempt/fence state, bounded evidence, pending generation, terminal_digest, and publication state;
- make duplicate prepared/terminal writes idempotent and stale claim settlement impossible;
- represent unknown/reconcile_pending without treating timeouts as absence;
- enforce bounded/redacted evidence and retention metadata.

The storage API must be consumable by bd-asx3 and the dispatch/reconciliation child without requiring either provider or host implementation. No new database, Nostr kind, MCP tool, or universal exactly-once claim.

Implements ADR-0032 Spec 5.2-5.4 as a standalone dispatch/reconciliation slice. Depends on the workflow-effect persistence child of bd-v139 and bd-htoo canonical identity; it may be developed independently of bd-asx3 host draining once the storage seam is defined. Own the effect state machine, attempt claims, lease expiry, provider capability/idempotency/reconciliation adapter, mailbox corr projection/link, crash matrix, stale-fence, changed-replay, unknown-outcome, and same-key retry tests. Do not own SQLite migration primitives, ApprovalSink/SpawnSink draining, terminal event publication orchestration, flywheel run ledger code, or br close.

Required behavior:
- commit prepared intent before dispatch and keep effect_key fixed across attempts;
- never infer no-send from timeout/process loss;
- settle only with a current fencing token;
- use provider idempotency/reconciliation policy before retrying unknown outcomes;
- link mailbox corr without making ReceiptStore the workflow journal;
- preserve the original failure and external uncertainty through compensation.

No blind retry, new runtime FailureKind, new Nostr kind/MCP tool, or universal exactly-once claim.

Implements ADR-0032 Spec 8.2 and the retention part of Spec 5 as a standalone host-effect slice. Depends on the workflow-effect persistence child and the dispatch/reconciliation child of bd-v139. Own separately journaled lease release/mail/comment cleanup effects, retry-safe cleanup outcomes, retention/compaction enforcement, redaction tests, and operator-facing unknown/cleanup evidence. Do not add a general undo DSL, claim rollback of completed external work, redesign sink draining, or edit br legal-close code.

Required behavior:
- cleanup has its own stable effect identity and never overwrites the original failure;
- interrupted cleanup remains pending/failed and can be retried idempotently;
- evidence is bounded, secret-safe, and retained only within policy;
- tests cover cleanup interruption, duplicate cleanup, retention expiry, and provider-unknown preservation.

No new database, Nostr kind, MCP tool, or universal exactly-once claim.
